  "worked_solution": "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$4$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$14$\n$-8$\n\n \n\n$\u2193$\n$4$\n$-12$\n$8$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-3$\n$2$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$",
